@synopsis
**yume-gui** [ **--kit** *name* ] [ **--page** *page* ] [ **--theme** *theme* ]
[ **--layout-direction** *direction* ] [ **--size** *size* ] [ **--no-tray** ]
[ **--yume** *path* ]
**yume-gui** [ window options ] **--capture** *directory*
**yume-gui** **--kit** *name* [ **--yume** *path* ] **--headless** *action*
**yume-gui** [ **--yume** *path* ] **--import-kit** *file* **--name** *name*
**yume-gui** **--version**
**yume-gui** **--completion** **bash**
**yume-gui** **--help**
@end

## DESCRIPTION

**yume-gui** is the desktop window for YUME clients. It imports sealed kits,
starts and stops each kit's man:yume(1), and shows what the running client
reports: its connection, traffic, local listeners, circuits, proposals of a
shorter route, printed lines and fixed security posture. It runs as an
ordinary user and needs no privilege.

Its tray icon shows the selected kit's state as a dot on the YUME mark: green
while connected, amber while connecting or waiting, red when the running
client reports a failure and grey when it is not running. The tray's menu
connects or disconnects the kit and shows or hides the window.

It talks to a running **yume** only through that client's control socket, as
**docs/protocol/CONTROL_1.md** defines, and runs the **yume** program to
validate, start and import kits. It starts **yume** as a detached process in
its own session, so closing or killing the window leaves the tunnel running,
and it stops **yume** with the control protocol's stop request. Opening the
window again finds a running client by its socket.

A kit is a directory under *$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/yume/kits* that holds a
*yume.json*, as **yume --import-kit** writes it. **yume-gui** starts
**yume --config** *kit*/yume.json **--control-socket**
*$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/yume/*kit*.sock*. A kit with a managed TUN adapter needs
privileges that this user process does not have, so run such a kit as a
system service instead.

The Connect page can rename a stopped kit, moving its directory without
rewriting the configuration or credentials and retaining its selection.
An existing name is never replaced. Both clients' control sockets are checked
before the move; an unanswered or refused socket prevents it. A configuration
with absolute references into the old kit directory must use relative kit
paths without parent traversal before renaming. A known client whose socket
has closed stays active until its process ends, including after the stop warning.

The Posture page states the protection the running client reports: the YTP/1
composition, the evidence profile its outer layers follow, its tuning limits
and the preset they match. It offers no switch that weakens a tunnel. A
proposal of a shorter route is accepted only from its review dialog, for that
exact proposal. Closing the dialog accepts nothing.

@diagram gui_lifecycle

## OPTIONS

@options
@opt **--kit** *name*
@cli spell: --kit <name>
@cli help: Show this kit first

Select the kit named *name* when the window opens. With **--headless** it
names the kit to act on.
@opt **--page** *page*
@cli values: overview connect logs posture
@cli spell: --page <page>
@cli help: Open this page: overview, connect, logs or posture

Open the window on *page*: **overview**, **connect**, **logs** or
**posture**.
@opt **--theme** *theme*
@cli values: light dark system
@cli spell: --theme <theme>
@cli help: Use light, dark or the system's theme for this run

Use **light**, **dark** or the desktop's **system** theme for this run
instead of the one chosen in the window, which is saved in
*$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/yume/gui.conf*. Light is the default.
@opt **--layout-direction** *direction*
@cli values: ltr rtl
@cli spell: --layout-direction <direction>
@cli help: Lay the window out left to right or right to left

Lay the window out **ltr** or **rtl**, mirroring it for right-to-left
scripts. Text in any script is shaped either way.
@opt **--size** *size*
@cli spell: --size <size>
@cli help: Open the window at this size, such as 1280x800

Open the window at *size*, written as width **x** height in pixels.
@opt **--no-tray**
@cli spell: --no-tray
@cli help: Show no tray icon, so closing the window quits the GUI

Show no tray icon. With a tray icon, closing the window hides it and the GUI
stays in the tray, and Quit in the tray's menu ends the GUI. Without one,
closing the window ends the GUI. A tunnel keeps running either way.
@opt **--yume** *path*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --yume <path>
@cli help: Run this yume program instead of the one beside yume-gui

Run the **yume** program at *path*. Without it, **yume-gui** runs the
**yume** in its own directory and does not search **PATH**.
@opt **--capture** *directory*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --capture <directory>
@cli help: Save every page as an image, then exit

Wait for the selected kit's first status, save each page and the route
review dialog, when a proposal is shown, to *directory* as
*page*-*theme*-*direction*.png, and the tray icon in each state as
tray-*state*.png, and exit. The exit status is 1 when an image
could not be written or a page raised a QML warning.
@opt **--headless** *action*
@cli values: status connect stop cycle
@cli spell: --headless <action>
@cli help: Act on --kit without a window: status, connect, stop or cycle

Without a window, act on the kit that **--kit** names, as the window's
buttons do, and print one JSON line per step with what the control socket
reported. **status** asks once. **connect** starts the kit and waits up to 60
seconds until **yume** reports **connected**, then exits and leaves the tunnel
running. **stop** waits until the socket is gone and the process has ended.
**cycle** connects, stops, connects and stops.
@opt **--import-kit** *file*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --import-kit <file>
@cli help: Import a sealed kit with the code on standard input, with --name

Import the sealed kit in *file* as the kit **--name** names, reading its code
from standard input, as the Connect page does. **yume --import-kit** opens
and writes it.
@opt **--name** *name*
@cli spell: --name <name>
@cli help: The name of the kit --import-kit creates

The kit's name: 1 to 48 letters, digits, dots, dashes or underscores, not
starting with a dot or a dash.
@opt **--version**
@cli spell: --version
@cli help: Print the version and the Qt in use

Print the product version, the Qt version in use and the control protocol.
@opt **--completion** *shell*
@cli values: bash
@cli spell: --completion <shell>
@cli help: Print the shell completion script and exit

Print the completion script for *shell*, which must be **bash**, and exit.
The script completes this program's options, their fixed values and file
arguments. To load it for one user, save it as
*~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/yume-gui*. This opens no window.
@opt **--help**
@cli flags: -h --help
@cli spell: -h, --help
@cli help: Print this help and exit

Print a short usage summary.
@end

## FILES

*$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/yume/kits/*name** is a kit. *$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/yume/*name*.sock*
is its running client's control socket, and *$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/yume/*name*.log*
holds what that client printed since this GUI last started it. The runtime
directory must be set, and *yume* in it is kept private to the user.

## EXIT STATUS

The window exits with 0. **--headless** and **--import-kit** exit with 0 when
every step passed, 1 when a step failed and 2 when nothing could be done,
such as for an unknown kit or a usage error. **--capture** exits with 0 or
1. **--help**, **--version** and **--completion** exit with 0, and a usage
error exits with 2.

## SEE ALSO

man:yume(1), man:yumed(8)

**docs/GUI.md** describes the pages, and **docs/protocol/CONTROL_1.md** the
control protocol.
